emsa-cv:35175f0d-bdd7-4e32-908f-17f7239e78fa prez:description """<h3>Contents</h3>
<ol>
    <li>
        <a  href="#enhanced-protocol">Enhanced Protocol</a>
    </li>
    <li>
        <a  href="#equipment">Equipment</a>
    </li>
    <li>
        <a  href="#instructions-and-procedures">Instructions and Procedures</a>
    </li>
    <li>
        <a  href="#additional-guidelines">Additional Guidelines</a>
    </li>
    <li>
        <a  href="#appendices">Appendix</a>
    </li>
</ol>
<h1  id="enhanced-protocol">Enhanced Protocol</h1>
<h2  id="field-collection">Field Collection</h2>
<h3  id="pre-requisites">Pre-requisites</h3>
<p>Pre-requisites for completing this protocol:</p>
<ul>
    <li>
        <p>Generate area checklists from Atlas of Living Australia,Australasian Virtual Herbarium or herbaria electronic floras, includingknown, threatened, and sensitive species from the project area.</p>
    </li>
    <li>
        <p>Become familiar with the characters and habitat requirements ofthreatened and sensitive species potentially occurring in the projectarea.</p>
    </li>
    <li>
        <p>Become familiar with the TERN Vascular Plant CollectionGuidelines.</p>
    </li>
</ul>
<h3  id="time-requirements">Time requirements</h3>
<p>Survey time requirements will vary depending on the number andexperience of personnel, their familiarity with the flora of the region,and the species richness and cover within the plot.</p>
<p>As a general guide:</p>
<ul>
    <li>
        <p>Allow between 0.5–1.5 hours to search for flora species andcollect plant specimen vouchers from the plot.</p>
    </li>
    <li>
        <p>Allow between 0.5–1.5 hours to prepare plant specimen vouchers,attach voucher labels, enter and scan the species into the app, andtransfer the specimens to a field press.</p>
    </li>
    <li>
        <p>Allow approximately 10 minutes per field press, every 2–3 daysduring the field survey, to inspect and change newspaper ifnecessary.</p>
    </li>
    <li>
        <p>Allow 1–2 hours after the field survey to deliver the plantspecimen vouchers to the local State or Territory herbarium.</p>
    </li>
</ul>
<h3  id="personnel-requirements">Personnel requirements</h3>
<p>Number of personnel and skills:</p>
<ul>
    <li>
        <p>Searching the plot and collecting a plant specimen voucher forall flora species present is best conducted with two personnel. Thisenables more ground, including microhabitats, to be covered frommultiple viewpoints.</p>
    </li>
    <li>
        <p>Processing plant specimen vouchers is best conducted with twopersonnel, one processing the specimens and one recording the datadirectly into the app.</p>
    </li>
    <li>
        <p>The surveyors making the observations should be familiar with andexperienced in identifying the characteristic identifiable features offlora species and how to distinguish one species from another. Ifsurveyors are not confident, time should be dedicated to practising,using field reference guides, and seeking advice before conducting thisprotocol.</p>
    </li>
    <li>
        <p>Floristics surveys involve the collection of plant specimenvouchers. Therefore, scientific permits are likely to be required.Additional permits may be required for collecting threatened floraspecies, so always check with your local authority.</p>
    </li>
</ul>"""^^rdf:HTML ;
	prez:label "Floristics - Enhanced Protocol" .

emsa-cv:5af5cef1-179c-4bae-9037-42196668deff prez:description "Rocky outcrops form when bedrock is exposed at the Earth's surface through erosion, a process which may take millions of years.", "The lithology type of exposed area of rock inferred to be part of the bedrock or unconsolidated deposits." ;
	prez:label "outcrop lithology" .

emsa-cv:6de1f874-fdcb-520a-9ff8-481a7968e76c prez:description "A juvenile is an individual organism that has not yet reached its adult form, sexual maturity or size. Juveniles sometimes look very different from the adult form, particularly in terms of their colour. In many organisms the juvenile has a different name from the adult." ;
	prez:label "juvenile" .

emsa-cv:6fd39a33-9c4f-469e-80a5-e76b5d5f04a6 prez:description """     <h3>Contents</h3>
<ol>
    <li>
        <a  href="#module-overview">Module Overview</a>
    </li>
    <li>
        <a  href="#introduction-and-background">Introduction and Background</a>
    </li>
    <li>
        <a  href="#key-definitions-and-terminology">Key definitions and terminology</a>
    </li>
    <li>
        <a  href="#rationale">Rationale</a>
    </li>
    <li>
        <a  href="#equipment">Equipment</a>
    </li>
    <li>
        <a  href="#instructions-and-procedures">Instructions and procedures</a>
    </li>
    <li>
        <a  href="#additional-guidelines">Additional guidelines</a>
    </li>
    <li>
        <a  href="#references">References</a>
    </li>
    <li>
        <a  href="#appendices">Appendices</a>
    </li>
</ol>
<h1  id="module-overview">Module overview</h1>
<p>This module covers the procedures and guidelines for recordingopportune observations of flora (vascular and non-vascular) and fauna(vertebrate and invertebrate). Fauna observations include sightings andcalls as well as indirect evidence of species presence such as tracksand scats.</p>
<p>Opportune observations are unplanned (no defined survey effort) andmade while undertaking other activities, at any stage of a project.Opportune observations often occur while travelling between monitoringplots and while undertaking other EMSA (Environmental Monitoring SystemsAustralia) modules. The likelihood of species detection is increased bytraversing the project area on foot and visiting the range of habitatspresent.</p>
<p>Opportune observations are entered directly into the Monitor app.Minimum data requirements apply to save a record, including taxonomicname or descriptive field name, location, date and time of theobservation, number of individuals observed, the observation method andthe observer. Additional information can be recorded using the optionaladvanced data fields. Records can be accompanied by physical voucherspecimens and/or photo, video and audio files, to verify speciesidentification. Voucher specimens are linked to a record via a uniquebarcode which is scanned within the app.</p>
<h2  id="relationships-to-other-modules">Relationships to othermodules</h2>
<h3  id="mandatory-related-modules">Mandatory related modules</h3>
<p>The Opportune Module is not dependent on any other modules.</p>
<h3  id="optional-complimentary-related-modules">Optional complimentaryrelated modules</h3>
<p>The Opportune Module has links to the following modules:</p>
<ul>
    <li>
        <p>
            <a  href="https://linked.data.gov.au/def/nrm/2b5c2281-e0f4-4ef5-8ae0-8ae745af9a7e">Floristics Module</a> – further details on vascular plant collectionand vouchering methods. Species names should be consistent between thesetwo modules.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>
                <a  href="https://linked.data.gov.au/def/nrm/1f2ac884-2177-403a-a6c3-27e109b1fc95"> Plant Tissue Vouchering Module</a> – provides standardised surveyprotocols for the collection of plant tissue samples and further detailson vascular plant tissue collection and vouchering methods.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    <a  href="https://linked.data.gov.au/def/nrm/0771a99f-27f4-4ff7-bf6d-aa70fe22ae51">Vertebrate Fauna Module</a> – provides standardised survey protocolsfor vertebrate fauna within plots, and further details on vertebratefauna survey, collection, and vouchering methods.</p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <a  href="https://linked.data.gov.au/def/nrm/0ad98270-707f-4a78-acd1-666faa2c124e">Invertebrate Fauna Module</a> – provides standardised surveyprotocols for invertebrate fauna within plots, and further details oninvertebrate fauna survey, collection, and vouchering methods.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <a  href="https://linked.data.gov.au/def/nrm/420ce0a7-9364-4bf4-861c-ef5f710e31b9">Targeted Survey Module</a> – opportune observations of species ofinterest (e.g. threatened or invasive) may trigger the need for targetedsurveys.</p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <a  href="https://linked.data.gov.au/def/nrm/cd04526e-7455-45c1-8a44-55575a4a7b2d">Fauna Ground Counts Module</a> – provides standardised surveyprotocols for ground counts of vertebrate fauna (namely pest fauna) andincludes similar data collection fields to those for opportuneobservations.</p>
                            </li>
                            <li>
                                <p>
                                    <a  href="https://linked.data.gov.au/def/nrm/8e7c86ce-e283-4cea-aca7-44ed7a22629c">Signs-based Fauna Survey Module</a> – provides standardised surveyprotocols for recording signs of vertebrate fauna (namely pest fauna)within plots.</p>
                                </li>
                                <li>
                                    <p>
                                        <a  href="https://linked.data.gov.au/def/nrm/dd2da1e0-edbe-4f20-a1ae-b860876c1837">Herbivory and Physical Damage Module</a> – provides a standardisedsurvey protocol for recording physical impacts of pest vertebrate faunaspecies.</p>
                                    </li>
                                    <li>
                                        <p>
                                            <a  href="https://linked.data.gov.au/def/nrm/f0123418-c565-45a6-be01-c5c332c727e5">Camera Trapping Module</a> – opportune observations of particular speciesmay warrant the use of camera trapping.</p>
                                        </li>
                                    </ul>
                                    <h2  id="field-collection">Field collection</h2>
                                    <h3  id="pre-requisites">Pre-requisites</h3>
                                    <p>Pre-requisites for completing this protocol:</p>
                                    <ul>
                                        <li>
                                            <p>Opportune observations can be recorded at any stage of a projectand are not dependent on other modules.</p>
                                        </li>
                                    </ul>
                                    <h3  id="time-requirements">Time requirements</h3>
                                    <p>Time estimates vary depending on the level of detail recorded andwhether voucher specimens are collected. As a general guide, allow:</p>
                                    <ul>
                                        <li>
                                            <p>2 – 5 minutes to record an opportune flora observation in theMonitor app, depending on if a voucher specimen is collected.</p>
                                        </li>
                                        <li>
                                            <p>2 – 10 minutes to record an opportune fauna observation in theapp, depending on if a voucher specimen is collected.</p>
                                        </li>
                                        <li>
                                            <p>5 minutes processing time per flora voucher specimen (barcode andpress).</p>
                                        </li>
                                        <li>
                                            <p>5 – 30 minutes processing time per fauna voucher specimen (e.g.tissue sampling, preservation, labelling, barcode).</p>
                                        </li>
                                        <li>
                                            <p>5 minutes per record to upload any photos/videos/audio recordingscollected outside of the app.</p>
                                        </li>
                                    </ul>
                                    <h3  id="personnel-requirements">Personnel requirements</h3>
                                    <p>Number of personnel and skills:</p>
                                    <ul>
                                        <li>
                                            <p>Opportune observations can be completed by one person but iseasier with two, so one can make the observations and the other canrecord the data directly into the app.</p>
                                        </li>
                                        <li>
                                            <p>The surveyor making the observations is ideally familiar with andexperienced in identifying the flora and fauna within the area.</p>
                                        </li>
                                        <li>
                                            <p>Scientific permits may be required for the collection of voucherspecimens. Land access permissions are required.</p>
                                        </li>
                                    </ul>"""^^rdf:HTML, """    <h2  id="key-definitions-and-terminology">Key definitions andterminology</h2>
                                    <p>
                                        <span  class="anchor" id="table-1">
                                        </span>Table 1. Keydefinitions and terminology relevant to the Opportune Module.</p>
                                        <table>
                                            <colgroup>
                                                <col  style="width: 11%"/>
                                                <col  style="width: 88%"/>
                                            </colgroup>
                                            <thead>
                                                <tr  class="header">
                                                    <th>Term</th>
                                                    <th>Description</th>
                                                </tr>
                                            </thead>
                                            <tbody>
                                                <tr  class="odd">
                                                    <td>Monitor</td>
                                                    <td>Field data collection app for Ecological Monitoring SystemAustralia. Collects data using the Biodiversity Information Standard fordelivery to the Australian Biodiversity Data Repository managed by theDepartment of Climate Change, Energy, the Environment and Water.</td>
                                                </tr>
                                                <tr  class="even">
                                                    <td>Opportune observation</td>
                                                    <td>A documented record of a species collected without standardisedfield protocol and without an explicit sampling design (van Strien<em>et al.</em> 2013), associated with a geographic location, date andtime; may be accompanied by voucher specimens, photos, video, audio andultrasonic recordings.</td>
                                                </tr>
                                                <tr  class="odd">
                                                    <td>Voucher specimen</td>
                                                    <td>
                                                        <p>A preserved specimen (whole plant or animal, part thereof, or atissue sample) that is retained for expert identification. Voucherspecimens are ideally deposited in a permanent and accessible storagefacility to enable examination and further study. See voucheringprotocols in the <em>Floristics</em>, <em>Plant tissue vouchering</em>,<em>Vertebrate fauna</em> and <em>Invertebrate fauna</em> modules.</p>
                                                        <p>The Opportune Module is not intended to cover the capture oreuthanasia of vertebrate fauna; thus, fauna voucher specimens arerestricted to those collected from deceased animals or observed signssuch as scats and hair.</p>
                                                    </td>
                                                </tr>
                                                <tr  class="even">
                                                    <td>Tissue sample</td>
                                                    <td>
                                                        <p>For vertebrate fauna, this is a sample of tissue taken from alive or deceased animal that includes ear clip/notch, toe clip, tailclip, skin, skin scrape (cetaceans), muscle, liver, heart, blood, hair,feather, eggshell, scale, faecal sample, egg or semen (Wildlife EthicsCommittee 2020). See tissue vouchering protocols in the Vertebrate faunamodule. The Opportune Module is not intended to cover the capture oreuthanasia of vertebrate fauna; thus, tissue samples are restricted tothose collected from deceased animals.</p>
                                                        <p>For flora, tissue samples ideally consist of approximately 10cm<sup>2</sup> of young, undamaged leaves (or the best/freshest growththat is on offer) that have been rapidly dried using silica gel granulesto preserve the DNA for subsequent molecular analyses. See also thePlant tissue vouchering module.</p>
                                                    </td>
                                                </tr>
                                            </tbody>
                                        </table>"""^^rdf:HTML ;
	prez:label "Opportune Module" .

emsa-cv:bc2ba93e-43f9-425f-83cd-bbf5c422bdf8 prez:description """<h3>Contents</h3>
                                        <ol>
                                            <li>
                                                <a  href="#standard-protocol">Standard Protocol</a>
                                            </li>
                                            <li>
                                                <a  href="#equipment">Equipment</a>
                                            </li>
                                            <li>
                                                <a  href="#instructions-and-procedures">Instructions and Procedures</a>
                                            </li>
                                            <li>
