emsa-cv:372ec5c5-79da-44a9-a56e-dd9ab311da06 prez:description "Brief history notes on the land-use where the study/survey plot is being described." ;
	prez:label "land-use history" .

emsa-cv:54f16769-034f-55ed-a26c-a0fb5d7b0ceb prez:description "Refers to the Major Vegetation Groups (MVG) as identified by the National Vegetation Information System (NVIS)." ;
	prez:label "Mallee Open Woodlands and Sparse Mallee Shrublands" .

emsa-cv:66aa8058-5e60-5025-a84e-de1234f23d8b prez:description "Woody perennial plant usually of the genus Eucalyptus. Multi-stemmed with fewer than 5 trunks of which at least 3 exceed 100 mm at breast height (1.3 m). Usually 8 m or more." ;
	prez:label "NVIS growth form - tree mallee" .

emsa-cv:9b841f72-4903-5d65-818f-d386a6eca15f prez:description "A tree can be defined as a large woody perennial plant with an elongated stem, or trunk, supporting branches and leaves in most species.", "Large woody plants, more than 2 m tall with a single stem or branches well above the base." ;
	prez:label "NVIS growth form - tree" .

tern:MaterialSample prez:description "A physical result of a sampling (or subsampling) event. In biological collections, the material sample is typically collected, and either preserved or destructively processed." ;
	prez:label "Material sample" .

ibra7-codes:MDD prez:label "Murray Darling Depression"@en .

tern-cv:2cf3ed29-440e-4a50-9bbc-5aab30df9fcd prez:description "The whole land surface is viewed as a mosaic of tiles of odd shapes and two sizes. The larger size is composed of mosaics of the smaller ones. Larger tiles (> 600 m across) are called landform patterns. Smaller tiles (~40m across) are called landform elements.", "Visible features of an area of land, including the physical elements of it soils, landforms (LandSurface Feature) , geology (GeologicFeature), water bodies (StreamChannel Feature), living elements of land cover (VegetationFeature), human elements (LandUse Feature) and transitory elements (Climate Feature)." ;
	prez:label "landform"@en .

tern-cv:5bf7ae21-a454-440b-bdd7-f2fe982d8de4 prez:description "A place in which study/protocol/sampling activities are conducted." ;
	prez:label "site"@en .

tern-cv:7e256d28-e686-4b6a-b64a-ac1b1a8f164d prez:description "Events that disrupts ecosystem, community, or population structure and changes resources, substrate availability, or the physical environment.", "In ecology, a disturbance is a temporary change in average environmental conditions that causes a pronounced change in an ecosystem. Outside disturbance forces often act quickly and with great effect, sometimes resulting in the removal of large amounts of biomass. Ecological disturbances include fires, flooding, windstorm, insect outbreaks, as well as anthropogenic disturbances such as forest clearing and the introduction of exotic species." ;
	prez:label "disturbance"@en .

