emsa-cv:0e0423c6-0dc3-40aa-9776-410a94299256 prez:description "Horizontal direction to which a mountain slope faces. Degrees from North. N=360, no slope=0.", "Horizontal direction/orientation to which a mountain slope faces. Measured in degrees from North- between 0° (level) and 360° (due north)." ;
	prez:label "aspect" .

emsa-cv:103f26cb-0194-45d2-8b1a-e18e1da6df2b prez:description "Vegetation classification that aligns with NVIS and is based on growth form, height, and percent cover." ;
	prez:label "structural formation" .

emsa-cv:132a1452-4fd7-473c-956b-3183dd315b67 prez:description "Slope class are a list of 8 classes derived from the percentage of slope - degrees." ;
	prez:label "slope class" .

emsa-cv:13f7d5bb-ac64-4f1e-b07c-68f8823a85ea prez:description "A program can encompass a range of activities, including projects, tasks, or interventions, organized and coordinated to achieve a common purpose. Some examples include, National Landcare Program, The Threatened Species Recovery Program, etc.", "A structured set of activities, initiatives, or procedures designed to achieve specific objectives or goals within a defined timeframe." ;
	prez:label "program" .

emsa-cv:148f60c9-92c5-4324-81b6-61b59bccdb4c prez:description "The difference in elevation between the high and low points of the landscape." ;
	prez:label "relief" .

emsa-cv:14b8c3fe-3a60-5f3a-bb7a-b9cf5df49b4e prez:description "State/Jurisdiction where the study is/was conducted." ;
	prez:label "New South Wales" .

emsa-cv:1b1c91ed-4a25-4ba6-9351-4048a308f936 prez:description "The dimensions of the plot; as a default the standard plot dimensions are 100 x 100 m (or 1 hectare)." ;
	prez:label "plot dimension" .

emsa-cv:325d5ff2-8c81-484a-a422-c13ff29666a9 prez:description "An estimate of the shortest distance from the plot centre/survey point to the boundary/nearest edge of the vegetation association/type different to the one that you are sampling." ;
	prez:label "homogeneity measure" .

emsa-cv:39b4421f-d610-5202-8ab6-42618837717d prez:description "Refers to the type of plot (i.e., 'core-Monitoring', 'fauna', or 'biodiversity', 'Control' or 'Impact') selected for a survey." ;
	prez:label "plot type" .

emsa-cv:3ea77e2e-038c-4ef6-b481-7af3a46291e4 prez:description "A checkbox field in the Monitor App to indicate that the slope is not uniform across the plot." ;
	prez:label "slope not uniform" .

emsa-cv:507d7e56-8e1a-4c9e-af03-5ba3b2b03374 prez:description "A bioregion is a geographical area, either terrestrial or aquatic or semi-aquatic, which harbours distinct ecological communities and not defined by any administrative boundaries.", "The Interim Biogeographic Regionalisation for Australia (IBRA) is a large scale regional classification of homogenous ecosystems, and sub-regions in the Australian continent. The latest version-7, IBRA7, classifies Australia's landscapes into 89 large geographically distinct bioregions based on common climate, geology, landform, native vegetation and species information." ;
	prez:label "bioregion" .

emsa-cv:58e4aba5-78c3-4361-b08a-ae39a93c8cb0 prez:description " Land surfaces characteristics that are in the order of 600 metres wide. Landform patterns generally have longer formation histories than landform elements.", "Any physical, recognizable form or feature of the Earth's surface, having a characteristic shape and is produced by natural causes." ;
	prez:label "landform pattern" .

emsa-cv:5b38b1ec-de00-51cc-a081-63f438ddf67f prez:description "Refers to the soil relief size class. Relief is the difference between the high and low points of the land surface. Landform patterns with extremely low relief are a Plain (such as pediment, pediplain, sheet-flood fan, alluvial fan, tidal flat, etc.)" ;
	prez:label "Extremely low ( <9m)" .

emsa-cv:632e4c3c-0e73-4b3d-b1d6-7b38897da5e8 prez:description "Any event that disrupts or brings about a change to the environment either by natural or anthropogenic activities." ;
	prez:label "site disturbance" .

emsa-cv:79e66285-9396-4b8f-a5ba-f957319bce9a prez:description "A state or province of a country.", "An administrative area with well-defined boundaries in a country." ;
	prez:label "state" .

emsa-cv:88068941-4dc9-4b44-bcfe-5eeeb3ba1a21 prez:description """<h3>Contents</h3>
                                        <ol>
                                            <li>
                                                <a  href="#enhanced-protocol">Enhanced Protocol<a/>
                                            </li>
                                            <li>
                                                <a  href="#equipment">Equipment</a>
                                            </li>
                                            <li>
                                                <a  href="#instructions-and-procedures">Instructions and Procedures</a>
                                            </li>
                                            <li>
                                                <a  href="#additional-guidelines">Additional Guidelines</a>
                                            </li>
                                            <li>
                                                <a  href="#appendices">Appendices</a>
                                            </li>
                                        </ol>
                                        <h1  id="enhanced-protocol">Enhanced Protocol </h1>
                                        <h2  id="field-collection">Field Collection</h2>
                                        <h3  id="pre-requisites">Pre-requisites</h3>
                                        <p>Pre-requisites for completing this protocol:</p>
                                        <ul>
                                            <li>
                                                <p>Become familiar with the standard terminology and definitions inthe Yellow Book and NVIS Australian Vegetation AttributeManual.</p>
                                            </li>
                                        </ul>
                                        <h3  id="time-requirements">Time requirements</h3>
                                        <p>Survey time requirements will vary depending on the experience ofpersonnel and their familiarity with the landform, land surface andvegetation of the project area. As a general guide:</p>
                                        <ul>
                                            <li>
                                                <p>Allow 15–45 minutes to collect and record theinformation.</p>
                                            </li>
                                        </ul>
                                        <h3  id="personnel-requirements">Personnel requirements</h3>
                                        <p>Number of personnel and skills:</p>
                                        <ul>
                                            <li>
                                                <p>One person is required to complete the Plot DescriptionModule.</p>
                                            </li>
                                            <li>
                                                <p>Personnel should be familiar with definitions of landform, landsurface and vegetation attributes, including but not limited to landformpatterns and elements, lithology, vegetation growth stages andvegetation structural formation. Reference material, including theYellow Book is recommended.</p>
                                            </li>
                                            <li>
                                                <p>Personnel should be familiar with and experienced in identifyingmajor vegetation groups and the growth stage of a system. If personnelare not confident, time should be dedicated to practising, using fieldreference guides, and seeking advice prior to conducting thisprotocol.</p>
                                            </li>
                                            <li>
                                                <p>The Plot Description Module does not involve interference withvegetation (no sampling is required) or wildlife. Therefore, scientificpermits and wildlife ethics approvals are unlikely to be required butalways check with your local authority. Access permissions will likelybe required.</p>
                                            </li>
                                        </ul>"""^^rdf:HTML ;
	prez:label "Enhanced Protocol" .

emsa-cv:8a4f71cc-7572-4b97-a3ef-c8061551b1fe prez:description "A unique name identifier of the selected plot, which is auto-generated in the Monitor App upon selecting the attributes in the following order: 'state', 'program', 'bioregion' and 'unique digits'." ;
	prez:label "plot name" .

emsa-cv:9a4421e0-c308-5e44-b778-24f2688ec1ea prez:description "Refers to the plant phenological stage characterized by active bloom, and peak flowering period.", "Refers to the plant phenological stage when in flower." ;
	prez:label "plant life stage/phenological stage - flowers" .

emsa-cv:9d4f8a2d-8e77-42dd-857b-0ecbc9c85696 prez:description "A landform element may be described by the following attributes, assessed within a circle of about 20 m radius: slope; morphological type; dimensions; mode of geomorphological activity; geomorphological agent." ;
	prez:label "landform element" .

emsa-cv:a2c2f997-46c0-5c64-aca0-a43e624cf8d5 prez:description "The age structure (or relative age class) of the vegetation in the plot, and can also be referred to the dominant growth stage of a vegetation (ages of trees and shrubs present) in a given region." ;
	prez:label "vegetation growth stage" .

emsa-cv:a6d4a219-7bbf-4017-91c6-106b4848480c prez:description """    <h3>Contents</h3>
                                        <ol>
                                            <li>
                                                <a  href="#field-collection-4">Field Collection</a>
                                            </li>
                                            <li>
                                                <a  href="#equipment-2">Equipment</a>
                                            </li>
                                            <li>
                                                <a  href="#instructions-and-procedures-2">Instructions and Procedures</a>
                                            </li>
                                            <li>
                                                <a  href="#additional-guidelines-2">Additional Guidelines</a>
                                            </li>
                                            <li>
                                                <a  href="#references-3">References</a>
                                            </li>
                                            <li>
                                                <a  href="#appendices-3">Appendices</a>
                                            </li>
                                        </ol>
                                        <h1  id="device-panorama-protocol">Device Panorama Protocol</h1>
                                        <h2  id="field-collection-4">Field collection</h2>
                                        <h3  id="pre-requisites-2">Pre-requisites</h3>
                                        <p>Pre-requisites for completing this protocol:</p>
                                        <ul>
                                            <li>
                                                <p>The plot must be established using the <a  href="https://linked.data.gov.au/def/nrm/d15d05db-5007-411e-b257-105ef4f76821">Plot Selection and Layout Module</a> prior to conducting the Device panorama protocol. The centreposition must be marked with a star-dropper.</p>
                                            </li>
                                        </ul>
                                        <h3  id="time-requirements-2">Time requirements</h3>
                                        <p>The time to complete the photopoints using the Device panoramaprotocol will vary depending on the user's skill level and experienceusing the camera. The density of the vegetation will influence the timetaken to locate the centre post. As a general guide, for each plot:</p>
                                        <ul>
                                            <li>
                                                <p>Allow 1 hour before the survey to pack the required equipment andcheck everything is functional, batteries are charged and theappropriate memory/SD cards are clear.</p>
                                            </li>
                                            <li>
                                                <p>Allow 20 minutes to take the equipment to the centre post, markout the triangle.</p>
                                            </li>
                                            <li>
                                                <p>Allow 10 minutes to take the three panoramas.</p>
                                            </li>
                                            <li>
                                                <p>Allow 10 – 20 minutes to pack up the tripod, tape measures, andstore equipment safely.</p>
                                            </li>
                                        </ul>
                                        <h3  id="personnel-requirements-2">Personnel requirements</h3>
                                        <p>Number of personnel and skills:</p>
                                        <ul>
                                            <li>
                                                <p>Photopoints is best conducted with two personnel but can beconducted by just one.</p>
                                            </li>
                                            <li>
                                                <p>The surveyor should be familiar with and experienced in theoperation of the camera. If surveyors are not confident, time should bededicated to practising, using instruction manuals, and seeking advicebefore conducting this protocol.</p>
                                            </li>
                                            <li>
                                                <p>Taking photos does not involve interference with wildlife.Therefore, scientific permits and wildlife ethics approvals are unlikelyto be required but always check with your local authority. Accesspermissions are required.</p>
                                            </li>
                                        </ul>"""^^rdf:HTML ;
	prez:label "Device Camera Panorama Protocol" .

emsa-cv:ac12fe2d-56fb-4e15-9fbb-6c2280b8585f prez:description "The spatial point within the plot where permanent markers (such as star droppers, tags, or pegs) will be situated, indicating that the plot has been permanently marked." ;
	prez:label "plot marker location" .

emsa-cv:b036ba09-d061-4a1c-99a8-890efc462a2c prez:description "The rise or fall of the land surface or the inclination of land surface to horizontal (expressed in percentage)." ;
	prez:label "slope" .

emsa-cv:dea45399-6c80-4abc-a24b-0e2b152c6800 prez:description "A checkbox option to indicate whether the plot will be permanently marked or not." ;
	prez:label "plot permanently marked" .

emsa-cv:e2396b81-e7cd-4ff4-adc4-d06e9643f592 prez:description "A unique four digit code provided by users in the Monitor App in conjunction with the parameters, such as 'state', 'program', and 'bioregion', to facilitate the automated creation of a corresponding 'plotname'." ;
	prez:label "unique digits" .

tern:Boolean prez:description "Class to encapsulate a true-or-false value" ;
	prez:label "Boolean" .

tern:Site prez:description "Location where observations may be made. This is a subclass of [Sample](#Sample) because a site should be designed to be representative of an environmental system (which may be an ecosystem or bioregion) or zone (which may be a zone such as a parcel or tract)." ;
	prez:label "Ecological Site" .

